<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chimera of Arezzo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chimera of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> is an Etruscan bronze statue of the mythical fire-breathing lion with a goat's head rising from its back and a serpent for a tail, made around 400 BCE. Found at Arezzo in 1553, it was acquired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> and is now in the National Archaeological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. An inscription on its leg marks it as a votive offering to the god Tinia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo.jpg" alt="Chimera of Arezzo" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chimera_d%27arezzo,_fi,_04.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
